%S A398784 2,3,5,6,9,12,16,21,28,36,51,64,87,113,153,200,267,349,461,601,795,
%T A398784 1033,1356,1751,2280,2942,3804,4888,6286,8032,10279,13076,16645,21079,
%U A398784 26705,33684,42470,53351,66971,83795,104766,130593,162628,201990,250602,310196
%N A398784 Number of integer partitions whose Bridge-and-Torch optimum crossing time is n.
%C A398784 a(n) is the number of integer partitions lambda such that BT(lambda) = n, where BT(lambda) is the optimum crossing time of the Bridge-and-Torch problem whose crossing times are the parts of lambda.
%C A398784 The standard Bridge-and-Torch rules are used: at most two members may cross at once; the torch must be carried on every crossing; when two members cross together, the crossing time is the larger of their individual crossing times; and all members must finish on the opposite side.
%C A398784 For a(n), it is sufficient to consider partitions whose sum of parts is at most 2*n. Indeed, every member must cross the bridge at least once, and for each crossing involving times x and y the crossing cost max(x,y) is at least (x+y)/2. Hence BT(lambda) >= sum(lambda)/2.
%H A398784 Sean A. Irvine, <a href="/A398784/b398784.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..72</a>
%H A398784 R. Backhouse and H. Truong, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scico.2015.01.003">The capacity-C torch problem</a>, Science of Computer Programming, 102 (2015), 76-107.
%H A398784 Göktuğ Omay, <a href="/A398784/a398784.txt">C program used to generate the sequence</a>
%F A398784 a(n) = #{lambda : BT(lambda) = n}, where lambda ranges over all integer partitions into positive parts.
%e A398784 For n = 1, the partitions [1] and [1,1] both have optimum crossing time 1, so a(1) = 2.
%e A398784 For n = 2, the partitions [2], [1,2], and [2,2] have optimum crossing time 2, so a(2) = 3.
%e A398784 For the partition [1,2,5,10], the optimum crossing time is 17: 1 and 2 cross, 1 returns, 5 and 10 cross, 2 returns, and 1 and 2 cross.
%Y A398784 Cf. A318271, A026791.
%K A398784 nonn,new
